# Analytics for your Appwrite Sites

![The Analytics card on a site's Deployments page with the last 24 hours of visitors, pageviews, bounce rate, visit duration, and top sources](/images/blog/introducing-appwrite-analytics/site-card.avif)

When the domain of a property is one of the domains of your site on Appwrite Sites, the two are linked. The site's **Deployments** page then shows an **Analytics** card with the last 24 hours of visitors, pageviews, bounce rate, visit duration, and top sources. A site without a property shows **Add analytics**, which opens the property wizard with the site's name and domain filled in.

# Privacy without cookies

Appwrite Analytics sets no cookies, and the tracking helpers keep no identifiers on the device. Appwrite identifies each visitor with a one-way hash of the IP address, the user agent, and the hostname. It uses the IP address to find the location and to compute the hash, and then discards it. The IP address is not stored with the event.

By default, the Web SDK sends no events for visitors who turn on Do Not Track in their browser. Keep personal data, such as names and email addresses, out of event names, props, and URLs. The [privacy documentation](/docs/products/analytics/privacy) lists everything that Appwrite stores for each event.

# Pricing and limits

| | Free | Pro | Enterprise |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Events | 50K per month | 100K per month | Custom |
| Additional events | Not available | $3 per 100K events | Custom |
| Properties | 1 per project | 5 per project | Unlimited |
| Data retention | 30 days | 90 days | Custom |

The events limit covers every property in the organization, including the automatic events that the tracking helpers send. Events from bots do not count. For the full details, see [Limits and retention](/docs/products/analytics/limits).

# Get started

Open your project in the Appwrite Console, go to **Analytics**, and create your first property.

